// Runtime/ManagedSocketRuntime.maxon — the `__ManagedSocket` box: one OS socket, closed on drop.
//
// ⭐ **THE ORACLE IS `specs/managed-socket.md`, AND IT IS THE ONLY ORACLE AVAILABLE**, exactly as
// `ManagedFileRuntime`'s and `ManagedDirectoryRuntime`'s headers record for their families:
// `run_program compiler=csharp` crashes (`E9001`) on a standalone `__Managed*` call, so the committed cases
// are the whole of what can be asked of the reference.
//
// ⭐ **HOST-ONLY ON THE SAME TERMS AS THE TWO FILESYSTEM FAMILIES.** `x64-windows` is real; every other
// target refuses at the call site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct` via
// `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, which matches on the `__ms_` PREFIX — so a method added later is
// gated by construction rather than by memory.
//
// ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE BOX IS ONE-LEVEL, WHICH IS `__ManagedFile`'s SHAPE AND DELIBERATELY NOT `__ManagedDirectory`'s.**
// A `__ManagedSocket` is an 8-byte box holding the raw OS SOCKET and nothing else. The directory family needs
// a second allocation because the OS WRITES THROUGH a 320-byte find record for the whole life of a search;
// a socket has no such record — every transfer hands the kernel the CALLER's buffer — so a second level would
// be an indirection with nothing in it. `specs/managed-socket.md`'s "Type Structure" states the single
// `_handle` field, and `SignatureIndex.managedSocketLayout` is what holds the front end's declared shape to
// the constants below.
//
// ⚠ **THE OPERATIONS ARE SYNCHRONOUS AND THE REFERENCE'S ARE NOT, AND THAT IS THIS TIER'S STANDING
// DIVERGENCE RATHER THAN A GAP IN THIS FILE.** The bootstrap runs `connect`/`send`/`recv` on an IOCP
// submission plus a pool of sync-fallback OS threads (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs`'s `__io_sync_op_net_*`),
// and registers each socket with the completion port. shv2 has no such pool: every `__ms_*` entry that makes
// a kernel call opens with `__gt_io_park`, the cooperative yield the `__mf_*`/`__md_*` bands already use, so
// the green thread genuinely suspends at the I/O point and others run — but the syscall is not overlapped
// with them. `GtIoParkName`'s header carries that argument in full for the filesystem bands; this family
// inherits it unchanged, which is also why the socket entries are NOT registered with an IOCP that would have
// nothing to complete into.
//
// ⚠ **"OPENS WITH" MEANS ONCE PER ENTRY POINT, NOT ONCE PER KERNEL CALL** — `emitIoPark`'s standing invariant,
// which this family did not hold until 2026-08-21 (`__ms_tcp_connect` parked four times; see the park site).
// `__ms_destruct` makes no kernel call of its own and parks through the `__ms_close` it delegates to, so the
// four parking entry points are connect / send / recv / close.

// ⭐ **THE BOX — its field offset and its size**, asserted against the registered `StructLayout`
// (`SignatureIndex.managedSocketLayout`) rather than agreeing with it by inspection, exactly as
// `ManagedFileHandleOffset`/`ManagedFileBoxBytes` are: the SHAPE is stated for the front end by that layout
// and written here by these constants, and two readers of one shape is the pair this project keeps finding
// drifted.
export let ManagedSocketHandleOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedSocketBoxBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// The handle value a CLOSED box carries — `ManagedFileClosedHandle`'s twin, and zero for its reason: the box
// is allocated zeroed, so a box whose `socket()` never ran and one that has been closed carry the same value
// and ONE guard covers both.
let ManagedSocketClosedHandle = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **`INVALID_SOCKET` IS ITS OWN CONSTANT EVEN THOUGH IT EQUALS `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`.** Both are -1 and
// they are two different APIs' answers — `socket()` reports failure as `(SOCKET)(~0)`, `CreateFileA` as
// `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` — so naming this one here states a fact about the socket API instead of making a
// coincidence load-bearing across two families. It is the argument `emitErrnoClassifiedThrow`'s three flag
// parameters already make one type over.
let InvalidSocket = -1 as ParsedInt

// `SOCKET_ERROR` — what `send` and `recv` answer on failure. Its own constant beside `InvalidSocket` for
// that constant's reason: they are two different API answers that happen to share a value, and one name for
// both would make the coincidence load-bearing across the two.
let SocketError = -1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE IPv4/TCP PROTOCOL SELECTION — ONE HOME, TWO READERS, AND THE SECOND READER IS WHY IT MOVED HERE.**
// These three values are `socket()`'s three arguments (spelled by `StdToX64Conversion.lowerOsSocketCreate`)
// AND the three `addrinfo` fields `getaddrinfo` resolves against (`buildManagedSocketTcpConnect`'s `hints`,
// below). They must AGREE: resolve for `AF_INET` and then create an `AF_INET6` socket and `connect` fails
// with a family mismatch — a wrong answer with no diagnostic anywhere. The x64 lowering's own header used to
// say these lived there "and nowhere else"; that was true only while nothing above the target tier had to
// WRITE them into an OS structure, which building `hints` and `sockaddr_in` in Std now does.
//
// ⚠ **A POSIX OR WASI LANE STILL NAMES ITS OWN `socket()` ARGUMENTS — what it may not do is disagree with
// the `hints` this builder fills.** These three happen to be portable (`AF_INET` is 2, `SOCK_STREAM` is 1
// everywhere shv2 could target, and `IPPROTO_TCP` is IANA's 6); an IPv6 slice adds operands rather than
// editing them.
//
// ⚠ **`AfInet`, `SockStream` and `IpprotoTcp` HERE — AND `SockaddrInBytes` BELOW — are declared WITHOUT a
// `ParsedInt` suffix**, and that is load-bearing rather than sloppy: this file reads all four as `ParsedInt`
// (an `emitConst` value) while the x64 lowering reads all four as `MachineWord` (`int(0 to u64.max)`, a
// `movRegImm32` immediate). A literal type widens into both; stamping either one here would refuse the other
// reader. ⛔ The count is written where the members are NAMED, because this sentence said "the four" while
// standing over three declarations, with the fourth eight lines down under a comment of its own.
export let AfInet = 2
export let SockStream = 1
export let IpprotoTcp = 6

// `sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)` — the length `connect` is given (by the lowering) and the size this file
// ALLOCATES for the address it builds. One fact: a box smaller than the length handed to `connect` is a read
// past the end of an `__mm_alloc`'d block.
export let SockaddrInBytes = 16

// `sizeof(WSADATA)` on Win64. `WSAStartup` fills it and nothing here reads it back — it is a scratch buffer
// the API demands, freed the moment the call returns.
let WsaDataBytes = 408 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **`struct addrinfo` ON Win64 — the HINTS this file writes and the RESULT it reads.** 48 bytes:
//
//   [0  .. 3]   ai_flags        [4  .. 7]   ai_family
//   [8  .. 11]  ai_socktype     [12 .. 15]  ai_protocol
//   [16 .. 23]  ai_addrlen      [24 .. 31]  ai_canonname
//   [32 .. 39]  ai_addr         [40 .. 47]  ai_next
//
// ⚠ `ai_addr` at 32 (0x20) is the one offset that is READ rather than written, and it is the bootstrap's own
// (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6270`, `MOV RAX, [RAX+0x20]`).
let AddrInfoBytes = 48 as ParsedInt

// The two WORDS this file writes into `hints`, named for the DWORD PAIR each one carries — `emitStoreDwordPair`
// composes two 32-bit fields into one 64-bit store, so an offset here names a word and not a field.
let AddrInfoFlagsFamilyOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let AddrInfoSocktypeProtocolOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
let AddrInfoAddrOffset = 32 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **`struct sockaddr_in` — 16 bytes, and every field is NARROWER THAN A WORD.**
//
//   [0 .. 1]  sin_family (u16, host order)   [2 .. 3]  sin_port (u16, NETWORK order)
//   [4 .. 7]  sin_addr   (u32, network order) [8 .. 15] sin_zero
//
// The two READ offsets are used against the address `getaddrinfo` handed back; the WRITE goes through
// `emitSockaddrInWord`, which packs the first three into one store for the reason stated there.
let SockaddrAddrOffset = 4 as ByteOffset

// The single word `emitSockaddrInWord` packs `sin_family`, `sin_port` and `sin_addr` into — the whole of the
// WRITE side of this struct, which is why there is one offset here and not three.
let SockaddrFamilyPortAddrOffset = 0 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **2^32 AND 2^16 AS SCALES, BECAUSE THIS TIER HAS NO NARROW STORE.** `accessWidthFor` carries `byte` and
// `word64` and PANICS for everything between (deliberately — a narrow access needs the `as` cast so its
// SIGNEDNESS decides the extension), so a `u16` or `u32` FIELD cannot be stored on its own here. Every such
// field is written by composing it into the 64-bit word that contains it, and these are the two multipliers
// that place a field at byte 2 and at byte 4 of that word. Named rather than spelled `65536`/`4294967296` at
// five sites: a bare power of two at a shift says nothing about which field offset it encodes.
let ByteTwoScale = 65536 as ParsedInt
let ByteFourScale = 4294967296 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **THE TEST-NET-1 SENTINEL, AND IT IS THE REFERENCE'S BEHAVIOUR RATHER THAN A TEST ACCOMMODATION.**
// `192.0.2.1` is RFC 5737's documentation range: it is guaranteed to route nowhere, so a real `connect` to it
// does not get refused — it STALLS for the OS's multi-second SYN timeout (~21 s on Windows). ALL FOUR
// reference backends short-circuit it (`maxon-sharp` x64 `X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6277` and arm64
// `ARM64CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:4730`; `maxon-selfhosted`'s `X64Backend.maxon:10744` `TestNet1NetOrder` and its
// wasm backend), and v1 states the reason in so many words: *"does not stall for the multi-second OS connect
// timeout"*. `specs/managed-socket.md`'s `connect-refused` case connects to exactly this address, and the
// spec suite's per-case budget is two seconds.
//
// The value is the address AS `sin_addr` HOLDS IT — network byte order, so `192.0.2.1` reads `0x010200C0`
// little-endian-first. It is compared against the resolved `sin_addr` word, never against a dotted string.
let TestNet1NetOrder = 0x010200C0 as ParsedInt

// The three "the call succeeded" readings. `WSAStartup` and `getaddrinfo` answer a plain int where 0 is
// success (`getaddrinfo` returns an EAI_* code otherwise), and `connect` answers 0 or `SOCKET_ERROR`.
let WinsockCallOk = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedSocketError` ORDINALS, AS ERROR FLAGS (`ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`)** — the
// `ManagedFileRuntime`/`ManagedDirectoryRuntime` discipline, and the ordinals are positional facts about the
// case order of the enum DECLARED at `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:163-172`. `verifyManagedSocketOrdinals` below is
// what holds these literals to that declaration.
//
// ⚠ **`bufferOutOfBounds` IS ORDINAL 0.** It reads like the rare edge case and it sits FIRST, so a
// transcription that "tidied" the order into failure-severity order would silently re-point every other flag
// by one — with no compile error, because a flag is just a number.
//
// ⚠ `connectionClosed` (5) has no flag here because nothing THROWS it: `specs/managed-socket.md` documents
// `recv` as *"Returns 0 if the peer closed gracefully"*, so a graceful close is a plain 0 RETURN and the case
// is the CALLER's to raise (`stdlib/TcpClient.maxon:69` does exactly that). The case is DECLARED — the
// ordinals after it depend on its slot — and deliberately unthrown, which is why its ORDINAL is here even
// though its flag is not, and why `claimManagedSocketOrdinals` states it as `neverThrown` rather than
// leaving a gap a corpus reorder could move in silence.
let ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketResolveFailedOrdinal = 1 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketConnectFailedOrdinal = 2 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketSendFailedOrdinal = 3 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketRecvFailedOrdinal = 4 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketConnectionClosedOrdinal = 5 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketClosedOrdinal = 6 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsFlag = ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketResolveFailedFlag = ManagedSocketResolveFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag = ManagedSocketConnectFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketSendFailedFlag = ManagedSocketSendFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketRecvFailedFlag = ManagedSocketRecvFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketClosedFlag = ManagedSocketClosedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// The `__ms_` prefix that identifies EVERY socket runtime entry point — the one test `scanRuntimeUsage` reads
// to set `usesManagedSocket`, the one `SemanticCheck` reads for E3104, and the one its yield roster reads.
let ManagedSocketRuntimePrefix = b"__ms_"

// ⭐ **THE FIVE ENTRY POINTS.** Four name a `__ManagedSocket` method — the surface BOTH references declare
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:1574-1583`) — and `__ms_destruct` is the compiler-INSERTED one: the drop
// a box takes when its last owner goes away (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`), which is the whole of
// the RAII half.
export let ManagedSocketTcpConnectName = b"__ms_tcp_connect"
export let ManagedSocketSendFromName = b"__ms_send_from"
export let ManagedSocketRecvName = b"__ms_recv"
export let ManagedSocketCloseName = b"__ms_close"
export let ManagedSocketDestructName = b"__ms_destruct"

// ⭐ **WHICH `__ManagedSocket` CALLEES THROW** — the composer behind `GtRuntime.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`.
// Held to the builders by the bidirectional drift guard in `installManagedSocketRuntime`, copied from
// `installManagedFileRuntime` for its stated reason: a predicate and a `throws` stamp are one fact in two
// places, and this project keeps finding that pair drifted.
export function isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	// ⚠ **THE TWO ABSENTEES ARE EACH ABSENT FOR A STATED REASON, not by oversight.**
	//   • `__ms_close` — `specs/managed-socket.md` documents `close()` as *"Idempotent. Also called
	//     automatically via destructor. Does not throw."*; naming it here would make a `try` REQUIRED on a
	//     call that cannot fail (E3055).
	//   • `__ms_destruct` — a DROP has no caller to hand an error to; it runs at a scope exit nobody wrote.
	return callee.equals(ManagedSocketTcpConnectName) or callee.equals(ManagedSocketSendFromName) or callee.equals(ManagedSocketRecvName)
end 'isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **WHAT THE USER ACTUALLY WROTE, FOR THE DIAGNOSTICS THAT QUOTE A CALLEE BACK AT THEM** —
// `managedDirectorySourceMethodName`'s twin, and its header carries the argument in full: an author never
// writes `__ms_recv`, so E3057 ("throwing function requires try") and E3059 must name the method they did
// write. It is a map onto the PARSER's own constants, not a second spelling, and it is asked only behind
// `isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee` — so the panic states a case nothing can reach rather than answering
// it. `installManagedSocketRuntime`'s drift guard asks it for every throwing `__ms_` builder it installs, so
// a missing arm fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`.
export function managedSocketSourceMethodName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if callee.equals(ManagedSocketTcpConnectName) 'tcpConnect'
		return ManagedSocketTcpConnectMethod
	end 'tcpConnect'
	if callee.equals(ManagedSocketSendFromName) 'sendFrom'
		return ManagedSocketSendFromMethod
	end 'sendFrom'
	if callee.equals(ManagedSocketRecvName) 'recv'
		return ManagedSocketRecvMethod
	end 'recv'
	panic("managedSocketSourceMethodName: no source-method arm for '{String.from(callee)}'. EITHER it is not a throwing `__ManagedSocket` callee — this map is asked ONLY behind `isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee`, so nothing else can reach an E3057 — OR that predicate gained a name and this map did not, in which case add an arm answering the parser's own `ManagedSocket*Method` constant. `installManagedSocketRuntime`'s drift guard asks this for every throwing `__ms_` builder it installs, so the second case fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`")
end 'managedSocketSourceMethodName'

// ⭐ **THE ERROR TYPE A SOCKET CALLEE THROWS — the WHAT to the predicate's THAT**, and the twin of
// `managedFileThrows`/`managedDirectoryThrows`. `ProgramSignatures.throwsOf` answers with it for a callee no
// file declares, which is what types an `otherwise (e)` binding and therefore what makes `match e` see an
// enum instead of a scalar; the three throwing builders stamp it as their `throws` clause. ONE speller for
// one fact.
export function managedSocketThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(ManagedSocketErrorTypeName))
end 'managedSocketThrows'

// The live OS socket of a box, or this family's `closed` throw — `ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow`
// with THIS family's three fixed facts supplied. Every `__ManagedSocket` caller throws
// `__ManagedSocketError.closed` from a box whose handle sits at `ManagedSocketHandleOffset`, so those three
// are a per-SOCKET fact rather than a per-call one; this is what keeps the triple out of `sendFrom` and `recv`.
function emitLiveSocketOrThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, join IrBlock, box ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: join, record: box, handleOffset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, closedSentinel: ManagedSocketClosedHandle, closedFlag: ManagedSocketClosedFlag, ids: ids)
end 'emitLiveSocketOrThrow'

// ⭐ **TWO 32-BIT FIELDS SHARING ONE 64-BIT SLOT, WRITTEN AS ONE CONSTANT STORE.** `low` lands at `offset`
// and `high` at `offset + 4`. It exists because this tier has NO 4-byte store at all (see `ByteFourScale`),
// so a DWORD field is only reachable through the word that contains it — and doing that inline would put the
// "+4 is the high half" arithmetic at every `hints` field instead of once.
//
// ⚠ Both values are COMPILE-TIME here, so the packing is folded into a single `emitConst` rather than emitted
// as a shift and an or.
function emitStoreDwordPair(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, offset ByteOffset, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let packed = emitConst(module, block: block, value: low + (high * ByteFourScale), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: offset, value: packed)
end 'emitStoreDwordPair'

// ⭐⭐ **THE `sockaddr_in`'s FIRST WORD — `sin_family`, `sin_port` AND `sin_addr` PACKED INTO ONE STORE.**
// The three fields occupy bytes 0..7 of a 16-byte address (`sin_zero` fills 8..15 and the block is allocated
// zeroed), and none of them is a word wide — so composing them is not an optimization, it is the only form
// this tier can write them in.
//
// ⚠ **`sin_port` IS NETWORK BYTE ORDER AND `sin_family` IS NOT**, which is the one asymmetry in the record
// and the one this function exists to state once. `htons` is the byte swap of the low 16 bits; `sin_addr`
// arrives ALREADY in network order (it is copied straight out of the `sockaddr` `getaddrinfo` filled), so it
// is placed and never swapped.
function emitSockaddrInWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, port ValueId, ip ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	// htons: `((port and 0xFF) shl 8) or ((port shrLogical 8) and 0xFF)`.
	let lowMask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0xFF, ids: ids)
	let byteBits = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 8, ids: ids)
	let lowByte = emitBin(module, block: block, a: port, b: lowMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let lowMoved = emitBin(module, block: block, a: lowByte, b: byteBits, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let highShifted = emitBin(module, block: block, a: port, b: byteBits, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let highByte = emitBin(module, block: block, a: highShifted, b: lowMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let netPort = emitBin(module, block: block, a: lowMoved, b: highByte, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)

	// `sin_family` at byte 0, `sin_port` at byte 2, `sin_addr` at byte 4.
	let family = emitConst(module, block: block, value: AfInet, ids: ids)
	let portScale = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ByteTwoScale, ids: ids)
	let portPlaced = emitBin(module, block: block, a: netPort, b: portScale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let familyAndPort = emitBin(module, block: block, a: family, b: portPlaced, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let addrScale = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ByteFourScale, ids: ids)
	let addrPlaced = emitBin(module, block: block, a: ip, b: addrScale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: familyAndPort, b: addrPlaced, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSockaddrInWord'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_tcp_connect(hostRec, port) -> (box, flag)` — RESOLVE, THEN CONNECT, OR THROW.**
//
// The sequence is `__io_sync_op_net_connect`'s (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6213-6350`),
// which is the reference that WORKS:
//
//   WSAStartup(2.2)  →  fill a 48-byte `hints` for IPv4/TCP  →  getaddrinfo(host, NULL, hints, out)
//   →  read `sin_addr` out of the first result's `ai_addr`  →  freeaddrinfo
//   →  the TEST-NET-1 short circuit  →  socket()  →  build a `sockaddr_in`  →  connect()
//
// ⚠ **EVERY FAILURE IS `resolveFailed` OR `connectFailed`, AND THAT IS A CONTRACT THE SPEC PINS RATHER THAN
// a convenience.** Both committed cases write `default panic("unreachable: tcpConnect reports only
// resolveFailed or connectFailed")`, so a third variant escaping this builder is an ABORT in a user's
// program. `getaddrinfo` answering non-zero is the resolve; **everything else — a `WSAStartup` that fails, a
// `socket()` that fails, a `connect()` that is refused — is `connectFailed`**, because with no Winsock and no
// socket there is no connection, which is exactly what that variant names.
//
// ⚠ **THE SCRATCH COPIES ARE FREED ON EVERY EXIT, THE THROWING ONES INCLUDED.** Four allocations pass through
// this function — the host cstring, the `WSADATA`, the `hints`, the `addrinfo*` out word — and each is
// released before the block that could leave. The `addrinfo` LIST is the OS's and goes back through
// `freeaddrinfo`, never through `__mm_free`.
function buildManagedSocketTcpConnect(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketTcpConnectName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedSocketThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var init = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msinit")
	var noWinsock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnowsa")
	var resolve = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msresolve")
	var resolveFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnsfail")
	var resolved = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msaddr")
	var refused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrefused")
	var create = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mssock")
	var noSocket = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnosock")
	var dial = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdial")
	var dialFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msconnfail")
	var connected = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let hostRec = 0 as ValueId
	let port = 1 as ValueId

	// The host arrives as a `__ManagedMemory` record whose buffer carries NO terminator, and `getaddrinfo`
	// takes an `LPCSTR` — `emitPathToCString`'s whole argument, one family over, and the scratch is this
	// function's to release.
	let host = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: init, pathRec: hostRec, ids: ids)

	// Winsock must be initialized before any other ws2_32 entry point answers. The `WSADATA` is write-only to
	// us: it is freed the moment the call returns.
	let wsaData = emitRawAlloc(module, block: init, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: init, value: WsaDataBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let startupResult = ids.mint()

	// ⛔ **`WSAStartup` IS NOT A PARK SITE.** It is process-cached behind `__net_wsa_init` in both references
	// and returns without touching the network on every call after the first, so a park here would report a
	// blocking operation that does not block. The two that DO are `getaddrinfo` and `connect()`, and each has
	// its own park below — see `emitIoPark` for why the unit is the blocking operation rather than the
	// function.
	module.appendOp(init, op: StdOp.osSocketStartup(startupResult, wsaDataPtrId: wsaData))
	emitRawFree(module, block: init, ptr: wsaData, ids: ids)
	let startupOk = emitConst(module, block: init, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let winsockReady = emitCmp(module, block: init, a: startupResult, b: startupOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(init, op: StdOp.condBranch(winsockReady, thenBlockId: resolve.id, elseBlockId: noWinsock.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: noWinsock, ptr: host, ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: noWinsock, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// `hints` — 48 bytes, allocated ZEROED, so only the three fields `getaddrinfo` reads are written. They are
	// DWORDs at 4/8/12, two to a word (see `emitStoreDwordPair`): `ai_flags`+`ai_family` in the first,
	// `ai_socktype`+`ai_protocol` in the second.
	// ⭐ **PARK 1 OF 2 — THE DNS RESOLVE.** `getaddrinfo` is the first of this function's two genuinely
	// blocking calls: it goes to a resolver over the network and can take longer than the connect that follows
	// it. The bootstrap gives it a park of its own by giving it a SYNC OP of its own — `SyncOpDnsResolve`,
	// which reports under the `net_connect` operation name
	// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6754`) — so the pair a resolve prints is spelled
	// `[net_connect]` there too, and this is the same pair rather than a new spelling.
	emitIoPark(module, block: resolve, operation: GtIoOperation.netConnect, ids: ids)
	let hints = emitRawAlloc(module, block: resolve, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: resolve, value: AddrInfoBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreDwordPair(module, block: resolve, record: hints, offset: AddrInfoFlagsFamilyOffset, low: 0, high: AfInet, ids: ids)
	emitStoreDwordPair(module, block: resolve, record: hints, offset: AddrInfoSocktypeProtocolOffset, low: SockStream, high: IpprotoTcp, ids: ids)

	// The out word holds the `addrinfo*` the OS allocates. Zeroed by `__mm_alloc`, so a failed resolve leaves
	// a NULL nothing reads.
	let out = emitWin32OutWord(module, block: resolve, ids: ids)
	let resolveResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(resolve, op: StdOp.osSocketResolve(resolveResult, hostPtrId: host, hintsPtrId: hints, outPtrId: out))
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolve, ptr: hints, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolve, ptr: host, ids: ids)
	let resolveOk = emitConst(module, block: resolve, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let didResolve = emitCmp(module, block: resolve, a: resolveResult, b: resolveOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resolve, op: StdOp.condBranch(didResolve, thenBlockId: resolved.id, elseBlockId: resolveFailed.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: resolveFailed, ptr: out, ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: resolveFailed, flag: ManagedSocketResolveFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// The first result's address, then the list goes back to the OS. `sin_addr` is a DWORD, so it is read as
	// the word containing it and masked — `emitLoadDword`'s reason, and the four bytes above it are `sin_zero`,
	// inside the same 16-byte `sockaddr`.
	let addrInfo = emitLoadField(module, block: resolved, record: out, offset: Win32OutWordAnswerOffset, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolved, ptr: out, ids: ids)
	let sockaddr = emitLoadField(module, block: resolved, record: addrInfo, offset: AddrInfoAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	let ip = emitLoadDword(module, block: resolved, record: sockaddr, offset: SockaddrAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(resolved, op: StdOp.osSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfo))
	let testNet = emitConst(module, block: resolved, value: TestNet1NetOrder, ids: ids)
	let isTestNet = emitCmp(module, block: resolved, a: ip, b: testNet, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resolved, op: StdOp.condBranch(isTestNet, thenBlockId: refused.id, elseBlockId: create.id))

	// Nothing has been allocated or opened on this edge — see `TestNet1NetOrder` for why the connect is not
	// attempted at all.
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: refused, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	let socket = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(create, op: StdOp.osSocketCreate(socket))
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: create, value: InvalidSocket, ids: ids)
	let socketFailed = emitCmp(module, block: create, a: socket, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(create, op: StdOp.condBranch(socketFailed, thenBlockId: noSocket.id, elseBlockId: dial.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: noSocket, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **PARK 2 OF 2 — THE TCP CONNECT.** `connect()` waits for a handshake with a host that may never
	// answer; it is the second blocking call and it gets the second park. `socket()` between the two does not:
	// it allocates a descriptor and returns.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE TEST-NET-1 EDGE NEVER REACHES HERE, AND THAT IS WHY `async-tcp.md`'s TWO TRACE CASES STILL PIN
	// ONE PAIR.** `192.0.2.1` short-circuits at `resolved` (see `TestNet1NetOrder`), so those programs perform
	// the resolve and no connect — one blocking operation, one pair. Both references answer identically for
	// the same reason: the bootstrap's `-2` sentinel comes back from the DNS sync op and
	// `maxon_net_tcp_connect` returns before its ConnectEx phase. A host that RESOLVES and then connects prints
	// two, which is what `specs/http-client.md` pins.
	emitIoPark(module, block: dial, operation: GtIoOperation.netConnect, ids: ids)
	let addr = emitRawAlloc(module, block: dial, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: dial, value: SockaddrInBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let addrWord = emitSockaddrInWord(module, block: dial, port: port, ip: ip, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: dial, record: addr, offset: SockaddrFamilyPortAddrOffset, value: addrWord)
	let dialResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(dial, op: StdOp.osSocketConnect(dialResult, sockId: socket, addrPtrId: addr))
	emitRawFree(module, block: dial, ptr: addr, ids: ids)
	let dialOk = emitConst(module, block: dial, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let didConnect = emitCmp(module, block: dial, a: dialResult, b: dialOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dial, op: StdOp.condBranch(didConnect, thenBlockId: connected.id, elseBlockId: dialFailed.id))

	// A refused connect leaves an OPEN socket, which no box will ever own — so it is closed here or it leaks
	// for the process lifetime, invisibly to the exit-101 gate because a socket is not memory.
	module.appendOp(dialFailed, op: StdOp.osSocketClose(socket))
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: dialFailed, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// The box: one 8-byte slot holding the socket, allocated through `__mm_alloc` so the ordinary ownership
	// machinery drops it — and the drop it takes is `__ms_destruct`, which CLOSES the socket before freeing
	// the memory (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`). That is the whole RAII half of this type.
	let box = emitRawAlloc(module, block: connected, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: connected, value: ManagedSocketBoxBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: connected, record: box, offset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, value: socket)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: connected, value: box, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketTcpConnect'

// `__ms_send_from(box, arrRec, offset, length) -> (bytesSent, flag)` — send `length` bytes starting at
// `offset` in the managed buffer.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`offset + length > capacity` THROWS `bufferOutOfBounds`; IT IS NOT CLAMPED.** That is the spec's own
// contract (*"Throws `__ManagedSocketError.bufferOutOfBounds` if `offset + length > capacity`"*) and the
// bootstrap's pre-check (`2-Parser.cs:13394-13404`, which passes `capacity` down for exactly this). A
// NEGATIVE `offset` or `length` is refused by the same guard: `send`'s `len` is an `int`, so a negative i64
// would arrive as an enormous count and read off the end of the buffer.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE BOUND IS `capacity@16` FOR AN OWNED BUFFER AND `length@8` FOR A NON-OWNED ONE — see the
// `pickBound` block below, which carries the measurement.** `__mf_read` states the neighbouring claim (*"a
// non-owned buffer fails it structurally"*) about its own capacity test, and that claim is FALSE on this
// tree for the reason recorded there; it is a defect of that builder, outside this spec's cases, and is
// reported rather than changed here.
function buildManagedSocketSendFrom(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketSendFromName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 4, paramTypes: fourI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedSocketThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(4)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschkoff")
	var checkLength = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschklen")
	var pickBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msbound")
	var viewBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msviewb")
	var ownedBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msownb")
	var checkCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschkcap")
	var outOfBounds = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msoob")
	var doSend = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mssend")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msfail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 4)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let arrRec = 1 as ValueId
	let offset = 2 as ValueId
	let length = 3 as ValueId
	let socket = emitLiveSocketOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: checkOffset, box: box, ids: ids)

	// ⚠⚠ **BOTH OPERANDS ARE TESTED SEPARATELY, AND THE ONE-TEST SHORTCUT IS A MEASURABLY WRONG GUARD.** The
	// tempting form is "`offset + length < 0` catches either negative", and it does not: `offset: 10,
	// length: -5` sums to 5, which is neither negative nor over any capacity ≥ 5 — so the pair sails through
	// and `send` is handed a NEGATIVE length, which its `int` parameter turns into a ~4-billion-byte read off
	// the end of the buffer. The sum is still computed once (the capacity test needs it), but it may not
	// stand in for the two sign tests.
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: checkOffset, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let negativeOffset = emitCmp(module, block: checkOffset, a: offset, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkOffset, op: StdOp.condBranch(negativeOffset, thenBlockId: outOfBounds.id, elseBlockId: checkLength.id))

	let negativeLength = emitCmp(module, block: checkLength, a: length, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkLength, op: StdOp.condBranch(negativeLength, thenBlockId: outOfBounds.id, elseBlockId: pickBound.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH FIELD BOUNDS THE READ DEPENDS ON WHO OWNS THE BUFFER, AND `capacity@16` ALONE CANNOT ANSWER
	// IT — MEASURED.** The spec's rule is *"throws `bufferOutOfBounds` if `offset + length > capacity`"*, and
	// that sentence is exact for an OWNED record, where `capacity@16` is a count. For a NON-OWNED one it is
	// not a count at all: a zero-copy view, an `.rdata` byte-string blob, an inline String and an immortal
	// record each stamp a NEGATIVE SENTINEL there (`BufferOwnership`'s four values), and the readable window
	// is `length@8`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND A BARE `extent > capacityExtent` DOES NOT EVEN REFUSE THE SENTINEL, WHICH IS THE TRAP THIS
	// BLOCK EXISTS TO AVOID.** The extent goes through `emitArrayByteExtent` → `emitElementByteLen` →
	// `emitBitsToBytes`, which ends in a LOGICAL shift right — so `capacity = -1` comes back as
	// `0x1FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, an enormous POSITIVE bound that every extent is under. MEASURED on this tree:
	// `sendFrom("ping".toByteArray().managed, 2, 9)` — a 9-byte read out of a 4-byte window — was ACCEPTED and
	// sent, and so was every larger one. ⇒ the ownership question must be asked with `emitBufferNotOwned`,
	// the ONE canonical decoder of that field, and never by comparing a byte extent against zero.
	let notOwned = emitBufferNotOwned(module, block: pickBound, record: arrRec, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pickBound, op: StdOp.condBranch(notOwned, thenBlockId: viewBound.id, elseBlockId: ownedBound.id))

	// The bound rides a block arg because it comes from one of two fields — the phi model is the only way a
	// value crosses a merge at this tier.
	let bound = ids.mint()
	checkCap.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(bound, argType: StdType.i64))

	let viewExtent = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: viewBound, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(viewBound, op: StdOp.branch(checkCap.id))
	viewBound.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(checkCap.id, argIds: argsOf(viewExtent)))

	let ownedExtent = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: ownedBound, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ownedBound, op: StdOp.branch(checkCap.id))
	ownedBound.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(checkCap.id, argIds: argsOf(ownedExtent)))

	// Both operands are non-negative here, so the sum is a real extent and cannot have wrapped.
	let extent = emitBin(module, block: checkCap, a: offset, b: length, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let overCapacity = emitCmp(module, block: checkCap, a: extent, b: bound, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkCap, op: StdOp.condBranch(overCapacity, thenBlockId: outOfBounds.id, elseBlockId: doSend.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: outOfBounds, flag: ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsFlag, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doSend, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let sendStart = emitBin(module, block: doSend, a: buffer, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let sent = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doSend, operation: GtIoOperation.netSend, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doSend, op: StdOp.osSocketSend(sent, sockId: socket, bufPtrId: sendStart, lenId: length))
	emitSocketTransferOutcome(module, block: doSend, transferred: sent, failed: failed, ok: ok, failureFlag: ManagedSocketSendFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketSendFrom'

// `__ms_recv(box, arrRec) -> (bytesRead, flag)` — receive up to the buffer's byte CAPACITY.
//
// ⭐ **A ZERO IS AN ANSWER, NOT A FAILURE**, per the spec: *"Returns 0 if the peer closed gracefully"*. Only a
// `SOCKET_ERROR` reaches `recvFailed`; `connectionClosed` is the caller's to raise from the 0 (see the
// ordinal block at the top of this file).
//
// ⚠ There is no length PARAMETER, and that is the reference's signature rather than a simplification: the
// method fills `managed` up to its capacity. A caller that wants fewer bytes allocates a smaller buffer,
// which is what `stdlib/TcpClient.maxon:62` does.
function buildManagedSocketRecv(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketRecvName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedSocketThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschkcap")
	var doRecv = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrecv")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msfail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let arrRec = 1 as ValueId
	let socket = emitLiveSocketOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: checkCap, box: box, ids: ids)

	// ⚠⚠ **A NON-OWNED BUFFER IS REFUSED BEFORE THE KERNEL EVER SEES IT, AND THIS IS A WRITE SO THE TEST IS
	// NOT OPTIONAL.** A zero-copy view, an `.rdata` byte-string blob, an inline String and an immortal record
	// all stamp a NEGATIVE SENTINEL in `capacity@16` (`BufferOwnership`'s four values) and none of them may be
	// written in place — one of them is a READ-ONLY image page. Handed on, that sentinel becomes `recv`'s
	// `int len` and the kernel writes ~4 GB through memory somebody else owns.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TEST AND THE BRANCH DIRECTION ARE `BufferOwnership.emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget`'s, NOT THIS
	// FILE'S.** `__mf_read` is the second surface that fills a caller's buffer from the kernel, and it made
	// the identical mistake this guard exists to refuse — including the `byteExtent`-based form, which does
	// NOT work: `emitArrayByteExtent` ends in a LOGICAL shift right, so a `-1` capacity comes back as
	// `0x1FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, positive and enormous. The measurements for both are recorded at that function.
	//
	// ⚠ It throws `recvFailed` and NOT `bufferOutOfBounds`: the spec gives `recv` exactly one failure variant
	// (*"Throws `recvFailed` on OS error"*) and documents `bufferOutOfBounds` as `sendFrom`'s alone, so the
	// other spelling would be a variant a `match` over this method's contract need not have an arm for. The
	// FLAG is why the shared guard takes a refusal BLOCK rather than choosing one: the two families' enums
	// name nothing in common.
	emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget(module, block: checkCap, record: arrRec, refuse: failed, fill: doRecv, ids: ids)

	// The buffer is OWNED here, so `capacity@16` is a real count and its byte extent is non-negative.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doRecv, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let byteCapacity = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: doRecv, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	let received = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doRecv, operation: GtIoOperation.netRecv, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doRecv, op: StdOp.osSocketRecv(received, sockId: socket, bufPtrId: buffer, lenId: byteCapacity))
	emitSocketTransferOutcome(module, block: doRecv, transferred: received, failed: failed, ok: ok, failureFlag: ManagedSocketRecvFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketRecv'

// ⭐ **THE TAIL OF BOTH TRANSFERS — ONE HOME, TWO CALLERS.** `send` and `recv` report failure the same way
// (`SOCKET_ERROR`, i.e. -1) and success as a COUNT, so what follows the op is one shape: test the count
// against the sentinel, and route to this operation's failure variant or to the success exit carrying the
// count. Only the flag differs.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT CAPTURE `GetLastError`, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE FROM THE FILE FAMILY'S
// `emitWin32BoolOutcome`.** Winsock reports through `WSAGetLastError`, not `GetLastError`, and
// `__ManagedSocketError` has no errno-classified variants to route into — the spec gives one catch-all per
// operation. Reading the wrong error API to fill a variant that does not exist would be a wrong answer
// dressed as diagnosis.
//
// `failed` and `ok` arrive from the caller rather than being minted here so the function's block ORDER is
// the caller's to state — it is what the emitted code's layout follows.
function emitSocketTransferOutcome(module StdModule, block IrBlock, transferred ValueId, failed IrBlock, ok IrBlock, failureFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let errorConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SocketError, ids: ids)
	let didFail = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: transferred, b: errorConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(didFail, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: failed, flag: failureFlag, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: ok, value: transferred, ids: ids)
end 'emitSocketTransferOutcome'

// `__ms_close(box)` — close the socket, idempotently. Returns nothing and throws nothing, per the spec's
// *"Idempotent. Also called automatically via destructor. Does not throw."*
//
// ⭐ **IDEMPOTENCE IS THE ZEROED HANDLE**, exactly as `__mf_close`'s is: the box is zeroed at allocation and
// this writes the closed sentinel back, so one guard covers "never connected" and "already closed" — which is
// what makes the documented "close then let the destructor close again" sequence safe rather than a second
// `closesocket` on a descriptor the OS may have reused.
function buildManagedSocketClose(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketCloseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doClose = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdo")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	let socket = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: box, offset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, ids: ids)
	let closedConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ManagedSocketClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	let alreadyClosed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: socket, b: closedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyClosed, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: doClose.id))

	emitIoPark(module, block: doClose, operation: GtIoOperation.netClose, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doClose, op: StdOp.osSocketClose(socket))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: doClose, value: ManagedSocketClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doClose, record: box, offset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, value: zero)
	module.setTerminator(doClose, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketClose'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_destruct(box)` — THE RAII DESTRUCTOR, AND THE REASON A `__ManagedSocket` MAY BE DROPPED AT
// ALL.** The refcount-checked teardown a box takes instead of the generic `__mm_decref`: last owner ⇒ close
// the socket, then free the eight bytes; otherwise lower the count and keep the box.
//
// ⭐ **A SOCKET IS NOT MEMORY, WHICH IS WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A SYNTHESIZED CASCADE** —
// `buildManagedFileDestruct`'s argument, one type over: `_handle` is a scalar `int` field, so
// `structFieldIsManaged` is false and `installStructDestructors` skips the type outright. Answering
// `__mm_decref` would free the eight bytes and leak the socket for the process lifetime, invisibly to the
// exit-101 gate.
//
// ⭐ It reuses `__ms_close` rather than re-emitting the close, so idempotence is stated once: an explicit
// `s.close()` followed by the scope-exit drop finds the sentinel already stored and does nothing.
function buildManagedSocketDestruct(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketDestructName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var drop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drop")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, func: func, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: box, lastOwner: drop, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(drop, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: ManagedSocketCloseName, args: argsOf(box)))
	emitRawFree(module, block: drop, ptr: box, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(drop, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketDestruct'

// The name this family's ordinals are transcribed under, for `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s diagnostic — one
// constant so the lookup's message and the claim's message name the same author.
let ManagedSocketOrdinalInstaller = b"the __ManagedSocket runtime"

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD FOR `__ManagedSocketError`** — the twin of `verifyManagedFileOrdinals`, which
// carries the whole argument: the case ORDER is the wire format, the declaration it is verified against is
// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s, and it is a free function because it needs the `Project` and must run on every
// compile.
export function verifyManagedSocketOrdinals(project Project)
	match declaredBuiltinErrorEnum(project, typeName: ManagedSocketErrorTypeName, installer: ManagedSocketOrdinalInstaller) 'declaration'
		notDeclared then return
		found(declaration) then claimManagedSocketOrdinals(project, declaration: declaration)
	end 'declaration'
end 'verifyManagedSocketOrdinals'

function claimManagedSocketOrdinals(project Project, declaration EnumLayout)
	var socketErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overDeclared(project, declaration: declaration, installer: ManagedSocketOrdinalInstaller)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketResolveFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketResolveFailedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketConnectFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketConnectFailedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketSendFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketSendFailedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketRecvFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketRecvFailedOrdinal)
	// A graceful peer close is a plain 0 RETURN from `recv`, so the runtime never throws this — see the
	// ordinal block at the top of this file.
	socketErrors.neverThrown(ManagedSocketConnectionClosedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketConnectionClosedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketClosedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketClosedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.requireTotal()
end 'claimManagedSocketOrdinals'

// ⭐ **INSTALL + THE BIDIRECTIONAL DRIFT GUARD**, copied from `installManagedFileRuntime` for its stated
// reason: the predicate above and the `throws` clauses stamped by the builders are ONE fact in two places,
// and a builder that gains or loses a clause without the predicate following is a `try` that silently stops
// being required (or an E3055 on a call that cannot fail). Checked in BOTH directions, and the source-method
// map is asked for every throwing callee in the same traversal (see `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`).
export function installManagedSocketRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if not usage.usesManagedSocket 'noManagedSocket'
		return
	end 'noManagedSocket'

	buildManagedSocketTcpConnect(module)
	buildManagedSocketSendFrom(module)
	buildManagedSocketRecv(module)
	buildManagedSocketClose(module)
	buildManagedSocketDestruct(module)

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(func.name) 'notOurs'
			continue
		end 'notOurs'
		let named = isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(func.name)
		let stamped = throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName)
		if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
			panic("installManagedSocketRuntime: '{String.from(func.name)}' stamps a `throws` clause but isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee does NOT name it — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted")
		end 'stampedNotNamed'
		if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
			panic("installManagedSocketRuntime: isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee names '{String.from(func.name)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted")
		end 'namedNotStamped'

		if named 'hasSourceMethod'
			requireQuotableSourceSpelling(managedSocketSourceMethodName(func.name), callee: func.name)
		end 'hasSourceMethod'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'installManagedSocketRuntime'

// Every callee this file installs — what the drift guard iterates, what `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set
// `usesManagedSocket`, what `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses on a non-Windows target, and
// what its yield roster reads. A PREFIX test rather than a five-way `or`: every entry point this file
// installs is `__ms_`-named by construction, so a sixth is covered without being remembered here as well as
// declared above.
export function isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ManagedSocketRuntimePrefix)
end 'isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee'
